# Read by the docker compose CLI, never by farmd.
#
# THIS FILE IS IN GIT. Nothing secret goes here: no DATABASE_URL with a
# password, no FARM_API_TOKENS, no FARM_NODE_TOKEN. Those belong in the
# shell that runs compose, or in the Helm chart's Secret. Compose reads
# the environment first, so exporting a variable overrides anything set
# here without editing a tracked file.
#
# That last sentence used to be false for the two names it is about. Compose
# forwards a host variable into a container only when a service NAMES it, and
# docker-compose.yml named neither, so exporting them reached the compose CLI
# and stopped there. Both are passthroughs now — FARM_NODE_TOKEN on every role
# and FARM_API_TOKENS on the api — so the farm profile is:
#
#   export FARM_API_TOKENS='<token>:operator:<you>'   # required; the api
#                                                     # refuses to serve an
#                                                     # exposed listener open
#   export FARM_NODE_TOKEN='<same value the node agents use>'   # optional;
#                                                     # without it recovery
#                                                     # tiers 3 and 4 and the
#                                                     # charge gate are refused
#   docker compose --profile farm up -d
#
# The demo service sits behind the "demo" profile so that
# `docker compose --profile farm up -d` cannot start simulated hardware against
# a real farm's database. This default keeps the laptop command a bare
# `docker compose up -d`. A --profile flag REPLACES this value rather than
# adding to it, and two --profile flags add to each other, which is what
# `--profile farm --profile metrics` needs.
#
# COMPOSE_PROFILES in the shell replaces this value verbatim, and it will
# honour a list: `COMPOSE_PROFILES=demo,farm` selects both, and then the demo
# and the api both try to publish ${FARM_PORT:-8420} and whichever container
# starts second dies with "port is already allocated". Nothing in the compose
# file prevents that; the exclusivity comes from typing one profile, not from
# the file. Pick one.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=demo
